  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate\", will return the same answer for the following two replies even though the rule requires different answers.\n\nReply A is the text: ```json\n{\"x\":1}\n{\"y\":2}\n```\n{\"z\":3}\nReply B is the text: ```json\n{\"x\":1}\n{\"y\":9}\n```\n{\"z\":3}\n\nFor Reply A, the fenced content contains two top-level objects, {\"x\":1} and {\"y\":2}; the rule says the last one inside the fence is {\"y\":2}, so the required answer is the dict {\"y\":2} (with no duplicate keys). For Reply B, the fenced content contains {\"x\":1} and {\"y\":9}; the last one inside the fence is {\"y\":9}, so the required answer is the dict {\"y\":9}. The rule therefore requires the function to tell these two replies apart, returning {\"y\":2} for A and {\"y\":9} for B.\n\nThe observation that would show this conjecture false is if the function returns {\"y\":2} for A and {\"y\":9} for B, or otherwise returns different answers for the two replies. If it returns the same answer for both, the conjecture is confirmed.",
